1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:02,400
A Friday, months after the money phone.

2
00:00:02,450 --> 00:00:05,880
Aziz is at the bazaar, and the bazaar is loud.

3
00:00:05,930 --> 00:00:08,928
He reaches for his phone, and his pocket is empty.

4
00:00:08,978 --> 00:00:11,544
The phone is gone, and the mobile bank is on it.

5
00:00:11,594 --> 00:00:16,944
From his sister's phone, he calls the bank and says one thing: lock everything.

6
00:00:16,994 --> 00:00:20,976
Who in this system is allowed to lock Aziz out of his own account?

7
00:00:21,026 --> 00:00:27,408
Lesson 18 ended with normal days: Aziz logs in on the same phone, with his password and a code.

8
00:00:27,458 --> 00:00:32,424
Lesson 15 sent one kind of question to a person at the bank: the phone review.

9
00:00:32,474 --> 00:00:37,800
This lesson opens the panel where those people work, and shows the four things that they can do there.

10
00:00:37,850 --> 00:00:39,672
Start with the people.

11
00:00:39,722 --> 00:00:45,720
Bank staff sign in to the panel only through the bank's own staff identity, with a second factor there.

12
00:00:45,770 --> 00:00:51,384
The platform keeps no staff passwords, so there is no staff password list in it to steal.

13
00:00:51,434 --> 00:00:58,104
A staff session ends after a quiet time, and after a fixed lifetime, and the bank sets both.

14
00:00:58,154 --> 00:01:03,840
Each staff user has exactly one role, and only the operator role acts on customers.

15
00:01:03,890 --> 00:01:08,808
The platform checks the role at each action on the server, not only on the screen.

16
00:01:08,858 --> 00:01:13,824
An operator finds a customer only with an exact phone number, never with a name.

17
00:01:13,874 --> 00:01:17,688
In this panel, an operator has exactly four actions.

18
00:01:17,738 --> 00:01:22,464
First, the phone review from Lesson 15: approve it, or reject it.

19
00:01:22,514 --> 00:01:24,816
An approval gives the customer level two.

20
00:01:24,866 --> 00:01:31,368
A rejection closes the customer at level one at once, and an SMS goes to the number.

21
00:01:31,418 --> 00:01:33,240
Second, the block.

22
00:01:33,290 --> 00:01:35,112
Third, the unblock.

23
00:01:35,162 --> 00:01:40,728
Fourth, a reset of the attempt counters, or of the mark that stops new MyID checks.

24
00:01:40,778 --> 00:01:47,232
Each action needs a written reason, and each action writes an audit event with the identity of the operator.

25
00:01:47,282 --> 00:01:49,104
Back to Friday.

26
00:01:49,154 --> 00:01:56,736
The operator types Aziz's number, finds him, and presses block, with the reason: phone lost, customer request.

27
00:01:56,786 --> 00:02:00,240
The block starts at once, and one operator is enough.

28
00:02:00,290 --> 00:02:02,160
Now watch what one block does.

29
00:02:02,210 --> 00:02:08,088
Every access grant of Aziz ends at its next request, as Lesson 17 showed.

30
00:02:08,138 --> 00:02:12,528
So the lost phone cannot move money, and it cannot even show a balance.

31
00:02:12,578 --> 00:02:15,384
Every request from his devices is refused.

32
00:02:15,434 --> 00:02:19,512
A login with his number is refused too, even with the right password.

33
00:02:19,562 --> 00:02:21,432
An open MyID check stops.

34
00:02:21,482 --> 00:02:27,120
But nothing is deleted: his devices and his verified identity stay as they are.

35
00:02:27,170 --> 00:02:29,448
A block is a pause, not an end.

36
00:02:29,498 --> 00:02:36,864
The platform sends the message account blocked, as an SMS to his number and as a push to his devices.

37
00:02:36,914 --> 00:02:41,424
It also writes an audit event and tells the antifraud system of the bank.

38
00:02:41,474 --> 00:02:44,616
A block is not the password lock of Lesson 11.

39
00:02:44,666 --> 00:02:50,400
That lock ends by itself after a time, but a block ends only when the operators lift it.

40
00:02:50,450 --> 00:02:55,104
So the answer to the first question is short: only an operator, with a reason.

41
00:02:55,154 --> 00:02:58,272
The platform never blocks a customer by itself.

42
00:02:58,322 --> 00:03:01,560
On Saturday, a stranger finds the phone in the bazaar.

43
00:03:01,610 --> 00:03:07,200
On Sunday, the stranger hands it in at the bazaar office, and Aziz has his phone again.

44
00:03:07,250 --> 00:03:09,072
He calls the bank one more time.

45
00:03:09,122 --> 00:03:12,504
The first operator asks for an unblock, with a reason.

46
00:03:12,554 --> 00:03:16,296
Nothing happens yet: the request waits as a proposal.

47
00:03:16,346 --> 00:03:20,352
A second operator opens it, reads the reason, and approves it.

48
00:03:20,402 --> 00:03:23,544
The operator who asked can never approve his own request.

49
00:03:23,594 --> 00:03:26,136
This rule has a name: maker-checker.

50
00:03:26,186 --> 00:03:30,192
One person makes the request, and a different person checks it.

51
00:03:30,242 --> 00:03:32,352
Only now does the unblock start.

52
00:03:32,402 --> 00:03:35,376
Each device gets back its state from before the block.

53
00:03:35,426 --> 00:03:40,104
His phone is the trusted phone again, the money phone, with no new MyID check.

54
00:03:40,154 --> 00:03:45,504
The platform sends the message account unblocked, and Aziz opens the mobile bank.

55
00:03:45,554 --> 00:03:50,280
The same two-person rule covers the approval of a phone review, and the reset.

56
00:03:50,330 --> 00:03:55,104
A rejection of a phone review gives nothing back, so it needs no second operator.

57
00:03:55,154 --> 00:04:00,312
Now a question for you: why does a block need one operator, but an unblock needs two?

58
00:04:00,362 --> 00:04:09,816
Because a block only takes access away and must be fast, but an unblock gives access back, and one tired or tricked person must not do that alone.

59
00:04:09,866 --> 00:04:15,384
Staff come in through the bank's own staff identity, with a second factor and one role each.

60
00:04:15,434 --> 00:04:21,168
An operator has four actions: the phone review, the block, the unblock and the reset.

61
00:04:21,218 --> 00:04:27,024
One operator blocks at once, and each action that gives access back needs a second operator.

62
00:04:27,074 --> 00:04:29,952
Every action has a reason and an audit event.

63
00:04:30,002 --> 00:04:34,440
The stranger held the phone for a night, and the push account blocked was on its screen.

64
00:04:34,490 --> 00:04:40,080
Lesson 20 shows what that push says, and why it says almost nothing about Aziz.
